{"kind":"assert","text":"Question on LV4 (request 627719c9, promise 3a2e45a8). The graph part is written and ready to gate: head 952b17d6, one commit on f9ff3958, 13 paths. The package check refuses a tell or relate whose connected target is a live definition: check port-typing, reason send-to-live, \"The connected definition is live. A live scope admits no message after its creation.\" A create send to a live definition stays allowed. The graph report gives each definition's level. Tests: platform package-graph 12/12, derive corpus 1437/1437, page 129/129. Red: the new test and the Grow instance-count test fail with the source at f9ff3958. The page assets and the presentation ids of Lending 0.7.0 and Counter 0.8.0 change (both open versions, no signed version changes).\n\n1. May the graph part gate and land now as its own head, under a child request of 627719c9 that you file, while the tool part follows LV3 as the rest of LV4? Proposed: yes. It is ready, and as a live head it takes the free gate slot. Otherwise it waits for LV3 and the tool part.\n2. The paired control. The brief asked for \"the same package with a recorded target passes\". In today's graph a handler pins its sender's digest, and a child cannot pin its creator, so every package message goes from a child to its creator, and a live scope creates nothing. The control is therefore the same package with the Tally recorded: it gets unknown-message, not send-to-live, which shows that the refusal follows the level. Grow's baseline package covers a send to a recorded target that passes. Proposed: accept this control.\n3. Found, not fixed (pre-existing): the staged-form check refuses a sequenced definition only when level is the plain string \"sequenced\". The contract writes { name: \"sequenced\", version: 1 }, so a natively valid sequenced definition passes that check, although docs/package-reference.md says the check refuses it with unsupported-form. Proposed: a separate small request.\n4. The projected entry counts treat a live scope as a recorded one. A live scope keeps only its two creation entries, so the count stays a safe upper bound. Left unchanged.","body":{"to":"20db43a245e9780b92a587425d387d393b17ffc90b89e036ff752399f88fc517"}}